  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1208 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nA shortwave is forecast to drop into the broad trough over the \nCONUS Saturday night into Sunday with its attendant surface low \npassing well to our south across southern Missouri. This places \neastern Iowa, northwest Illinois, and far northeast Missouri on \nthe northern edge of the system. Recent models runs have trended\nfaster with this storm system with the rain coming to an end \nSunday morning. The cold front will move through Sunday morning\nwith the warmest part of the day in the morning with \ntemperatures holding steady in the upper 30s to lower 40s during\nthe day. Rainfall amounts between a quarter of an inch and half\nan inch are possible (NBM probs >0.25 of 20 to 40%), mainly \nacross the southern half of the area. Rain may mix with snow as \nprecipitation comes to an end Sunday morning. It will also be \nbreezy post frontal with northwest winds of 10 to 20 MPH and \ngusts up to 30 MPH. \n\nSunday night onward, zonal flow is forecast to be in place across \nthe CONUS through the remainder of the period. Models show a \nshortwave moving across the region on Tuesday but moisture is\nlimited and this system looks to only lead to increasing cloud\ncover. Late in the period, models show a closed 500 MB low and\ntrough moving into the southwest US with ridging building \nacross the Plains with gradually warming temperatures especially\nTuesday through Wednesday with temperatures potentially warming\ninto the mid to upper 50s.",
